WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:02.880
Speaker 1: It's great to welcome back to the
program today.

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:08.889
Mike Rinder, former senior executive of the
Church of Scientology and the C organization,

00:00:08.889 --> 00:00:13.900
also author of the new book A Billion Years
My Escape from a Life in the Highest Ranks

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:15.320
of Scientology.

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:17.619
Mike, great to have you on again, as always.

00:00:17.619 --> 00:00:18.869
Speaker 5: Thanks, David.

00:00:18.869 --> 00:00:19.930
It's wonderful to be back.

00:00:19.930 --> 00:00:24.699
Speaker 1: So, I mean, in the interests of
treading some new ground right up front since

00:00:24.699 --> 00:00:28.090
the last time you've been here, you know,
a lot of things have happened in the world

00:00:28.090 --> 00:00:31.369
that don't relate directly to Scientology.

00:00:31.369 --> 00:00:38.090
I think there's this feeling among some that
the Church of Scientology right now is somewhat

00:00:38.090 --> 00:00:44.899
diminished, both in notoriety and maybe in
strength, because it's not really in the news

00:00:44.899 --> 00:00:46.610
as much as it once was.

00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:49.230
Is that a fair assessment or do you disagree
with that?

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:52.680
Speaker 5: Oh, no, I think that's a very fair
assessment, David.

00:00:52.680 --> 00:01:03.910
I think that Scientology has been dwindling
as to its overall numbers and its influence,

00:01:03.910 --> 00:01:09.470
primarily due to the Internet, also due to
a lot of media coverage.

00:01:09.470 --> 00:01:16.630
You know, information is the enemy of high
control organizations and groups and cults,

00:01:16.630 --> 00:01:23.130
and information is available to anybody that
has a computer or a phone and can Google.

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:27.340
And, you know, these days, nobody buys a pair
of shoes without Googling.

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:29.380
These are the best ones.

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:30.380
Yeah.

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:34.909
So it's very difficult for Scientology to.

00:01:34.909 --> 00:01:37.119
Get new people in.

00:01:37.119 --> 00:01:38.260
And so.

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:44.680
Their numbers are diminishing, but I wouldn't
say that that is diminishing their revenues

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:52.640
yet, because what happens is you get organizations
that are, you know, in a bubble of us versus

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:54.470
them mentality.

00:01:54.470 --> 00:01:59.119
When the walls start closing in, the people
inside feel more desperate that they have

00:01:59.119 --> 00:02:06.789
to do more, give more money, but like become
more active and involved in order to save

00:02:06.789 --> 00:02:10.729
their particular cult from extinction.

00:02:10.729 --> 00:02:18.950
So probably Scientology is still raking in,
you know, millions and millions a week, but

00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:25.569
that is an unsustainable model because eventually
those people run dry and you have to have

00:02:25.569 --> 00:02:28.820
new people coming in and that is not happening.

00:02:28.820 --> 00:02:34.200
And, you know, you add to that the problems
that have come up.

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:39.970
You know, there's been this political race
for the mayor's office in Los Angeles, which

00:02:39.970 --> 00:02:45.220
is turned into a mudslinging contest on who
can spit on Scientology.

00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:50.330
The biggest and the Danny Masterson trial
now started.

00:02:50.330 --> 00:02:57.209
A lot of things are looking pretty dark in
the world of Scientology right about now.

00:02:57.209 --> 00:03:04.319
Speaker 1: In terms of the numbers, you know,
I in Lawrence Wright's book Going Clear, Wright

00:03:04.319 --> 00:03:10.180
writes that internal claims often allude to
millions of members.

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:15.440
I've read numbers as small as 30,000 globally,
which which I don't even know.

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:17.920
It sounds very low, but maybe it is that low.

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:20.190
What's your sense of the membership at this
point?

00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:24.280
Speaker 5: Well, my estimate at this point
is around 25,000.

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:25.920
Speaker 1: Oh, wow.

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:27.590
That's the lowest I've heard anyone even state.

00:03:27.590 --> 00:03:32.510
Speaker 5: Well, there's a few others like
who have that similar opinion.

00:03:32.510 --> 00:03:37.530
Aaron Smith, Levin and Chris Shelton and Jeffrey
Hawkins.

00:03:37.530 --> 00:03:43.739
Jefferson Hawkins, all of whom are former
Sea Org people, you know, high level executives

00:03:43.739 --> 00:03:50.700
in Scientology who had access in one way or
another to information about membership.

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:58.170
Scientology inflates their membership figures
by claiming that anybody who has ever purchased

00:03:58.170 --> 00:04:03.819
a Scientology or Dianetics book therefore
becomes a member because we have them name

00:04:03.819 --> 00:04:06.620
and address from when they purchased the book.

00:04:06.620 --> 00:04:10.670
And over 50 years there's been millions of
people who purchase them.

00:04:10.670 --> 00:04:14.439
So ergo, there are millions of Scientologists.

00:04:14.439 --> 00:04:20.320
But if you look and see things like.

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:26.610
Scientology claims to have 15,000 members
in the Tampa Bay area or in Clearwater.

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:27.610
Right.

00:04:27.610 --> 00:04:29.259
You know, the numbers vary.

00:04:29.259 --> 00:04:39.270
But when Mark Bunker, who was a very avowed
opponent of Scientology, ran for city council,

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:41.949
the Clearwater City Council.

00:04:41.949 --> 00:04:49.220
He won with 5000 votes so they couldn't round
up enough votes to prevent Mark Bunker in

00:04:49.220 --> 00:04:56.139
the, you know, enclave of the highest concentration
of Scientologists anywhere in the world.

00:04:56.139 --> 00:05:00.850
So that pretty much put paid to any claim
that they have millions of members.

00:05:00.850 --> 00:05:03.280
They may never have had millions.

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:08.100
They may have had 100,000 or a couple of hundred
thousand back in the seventies or the eighties.

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:10.170
But today, no way.

00:05:10.170 --> 00:05:16.440
Speaker 1: You you escaped in 2007, if I recall
correctly, which is roughly like 15, getting

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:18.490
closer to six years ago.

00:05:18.490 --> 00:05:23.030
Do you think that you were still on the radar
of the church?

00:05:23.030 --> 00:05:24.240
Are you being tracked?

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:28.720
How closely do they I mean, like will people
from the church see this interview, for example,

00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:29.910
that we're doing right now?

00:05:29.910 --> 00:05:30.910
Speaker 5: Absolutely.

00:05:30.910 --> 00:05:34.850
David, you'll probably get a letter from a
lawyer before you manage to put this up on

00:05:34.850 --> 00:05:35.850
anything.

00:05:35.850 --> 00:05:36.850
Oh, boy.

00:05:36.850 --> 00:05:37.850
But but.

00:05:37.850 --> 00:05:40.669
Speaker 1: Well, it didn't happen with the
last one.

00:05:40.669 --> 00:05:41.669
Hopefully.

00:05:41.669 --> 00:05:42.669
Hopefully not this time.

00:05:42.669 --> 00:05:45.740
Speaker 5: Yeah, but now my book is out, so
they're going a little crazy.

00:05:45.740 --> 00:05:47.510
Yeah, they're.

00:05:47.510 --> 00:05:54.060
They're paying for Google ads, so any search
of my name shows up with a Scientology Google

00:05:54.060 --> 00:06:01.151
ad putting up new smear videos and smear sites
and sending legal threat letters and, you

00:06:01.151 --> 00:06:05.360
know, all the usual shenanigans that Scientology
gets up to.

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:08.430
I'm certainly not gone off their radar.

00:06:08.430 --> 00:06:14.250
In fact, I came like right back into the center
of it the day my book was released.

00:06:14.250 --> 00:06:19.949
Speaker 1: One of the things I think is interesting
about your story, and for people who want

00:06:19.949 --> 00:06:24.979
the details of how you ultimately kind of
changed your mind about the Church of Scientology

00:06:24.979 --> 00:06:29.139
and ultimately left, we go into that in detail
in our first interview.

00:06:29.139 --> 00:06:34.850
One of the interesting conversations we're
having now is generally about the deprogramming,

00:06:34.850 --> 00:06:38.220
as the term sometimes is, of people who are
in a cult.

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:41.940
It might not come as a shock to you that many
in my audience believe that Trumpism is a

00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:47.479
cult, and they see the people at the Trump
rallies with impossible stories and contradictory

00:06:47.479 --> 00:06:53.919
beliefs and an inability to ever kind of puncture
the bubble in which they exist.

00:06:53.919 --> 00:06:59.340
And the question is, okay, so like what can
be done to accelerate or help people along

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:01.139
in in that process?

00:07:01.139 --> 00:07:06.590
What have you learned in your experience about
what can be done from outside the cult to

00:07:06.590 --> 00:07:10.639
help people in it see the truth or something
closer to the truth?

00:07:10.639 --> 00:07:14.919
Speaker 5: Well, from my experience, it's
very tough.

00:07:14.919 --> 00:07:17.870
It is very tough to penetrate that barrier.

00:07:17.870 --> 00:07:27.150
But in the case of Scientology, what I tell
people to do is use the words of L. Ron Hubbard

00:07:27.150 --> 00:07:30.500
as the entrance point to get them to look.

00:07:30.500 --> 00:07:35.120
You know, Hubbard said a lot of things, many
of which are contradictory.

00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:39.990
And on one hand, he said, you know, do everything
that I say and read and follow it.

00:07:39.990 --> 00:07:40.990
Exactly.

00:07:40.990 --> 00:07:42.620
On the other hand, he said, believe what?

00:07:42.620 --> 00:07:44.440
You know what is true for you.

00:07:44.440 --> 00:07:50.889
And look, don't listen and, you know, think
for yourself, all of which are very contradictory

00:07:50.889 --> 00:07:53.259
to the actual teachings of Scientology.

00:07:53.259 --> 00:08:00.379
But if you quote those things to a Scientologist
and say, look, Hubbard says, you've got to

00:08:00.379 --> 00:08:01.440
think for yourself.

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:03.440
Hubbard says, you've got to look.

00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:04.440
Don't listen.

00:08:04.440 --> 00:08:08.950
Hubbard says what's real for you is what's
real for you, not what someone tells you is

00:08:08.950 --> 00:08:09.950
real.

00:08:09.950 --> 00:08:14.949
So don't you have an obligation to follow
what Hubbard says and actually look at information

00:08:14.949 --> 00:08:20.639
outside of that bubble that has limited success?

00:08:20.639 --> 00:08:23.210
Yeah, I don't know, David.

00:08:23.210 --> 00:08:28.030
Anything that actually is a guaranteed silver
bullet.

00:08:28.030 --> 00:08:32.680
There's no no garlic for this vampire that
works 100% of the time.

00:08:32.680 --> 00:08:36.459
It just is sort of.

00:08:36.459 --> 00:08:41.339
The one thing I do know that doesn't work
is to tell them they're stupid.

00:08:41.339 --> 00:08:42.339
Right.

00:08:42.339 --> 00:08:43.339
Tell them that.

00:08:43.339 --> 00:08:51.470
That they're completely ignorant and stupid
and that they just are fools because that

00:08:51.470 --> 00:08:57.350
plays into their own stereotype that the rest
of the world are the ones who don't know what's

00:08:57.350 --> 00:08:58.350
really going on.

00:08:58.350 --> 00:09:02.860
Speaker 1: The other thing that outside not
as applied specifically to Scientology, but

00:09:02.860 --> 00:09:08.170
that's often mentioned by critical thinking
folks and skeptics and this type of thing.

00:09:08.170 --> 00:09:12.490
Is that just like bashing people over the
head with facts also isn't very effective.

00:09:12.490 --> 00:09:16.930
So like as applied to Scientology, that would
be like explaining the scientific impossibility

00:09:16.930 --> 00:09:19.960
of some of the origin story that's told or
whatever.

00:09:19.960 --> 00:09:24.760
Is your experience also that talking about
the impossibility of that is not effective?

00:09:24.760 --> 00:09:25.930
Speaker 5: Yes.

00:09:25.930 --> 00:09:28.500
Of that that that is useless.

00:09:28.500 --> 00:09:36.960
You can't approach someone who has beliefs
and try and undermine their beliefs.

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:45.530
The only thing that I have seen that works
is the demonstration of the acts in the real

00:09:45.530 --> 00:09:50.329
world that are contradictory to those beliefs.

00:09:50.329 --> 00:09:51.329
Hmm.

00:09:51.329 --> 00:09:58.370
Like, if you take someone and say you can't
believe that there is a virgin birth, then

00:09:58.370 --> 00:10:01.769
you're going to have a real hard time with
most Christians convincing them that there

00:10:01.769 --> 00:10:07.220
is no such thing as a virgin birth because
they believe that that is the case.

00:10:07.220 --> 00:10:17.040
If you say that they're look, this this priest
molested this child and has been convicted

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:20.130
of that and the church covered it up.

00:10:20.130 --> 00:10:26.320
It's a much harder thing to overcome that
and just go, well, I don't really care because

00:10:26.320 --> 00:10:29.149
I believe in the Bible.

00:10:29.149 --> 00:10:36.880
So you got to kind of find the things that
are demonstrable in the real world that are.

00:10:36.880 --> 00:10:40.690
And but, you know, even that doesn't work
most of the time.

00:10:40.690 --> 00:10:47.470
I mean, the justifications and explanations
as to why things are the way they are or how

00:10:47.470 --> 00:10:52.210
they're rationalized can be pretty, pretty
out there.

00:10:52.210 --> 00:10:56.990
I mean, I see these people and, you know,
I watch your channel and a bunch of other

00:10:56.990 --> 00:11:05.200
stuff and I see people the, you know, JFK
Jr is alive and well and like, you know.

00:11:05.200 --> 00:11:06.540
Yeah.

00:11:06.540 --> 00:11:07.540
Like what?

00:11:07.540 --> 00:11:10.920
Speaker 1: So one of the.

00:11:10.920 --> 00:11:17.440
One of the things that's commonly told by
people who were sucked into organized crime

00:11:17.440 --> 00:11:23.690
is that the way that they ended up all the
way in started relatively innocuously.

00:11:23.690 --> 00:11:29.430
So it might start with, hey, can you just
run this envelope across town for me?

00:11:29.430 --> 00:11:34.990
And there's this kind of process through which
your before you know it, you're much further

00:11:34.990 --> 00:11:39.810
in than maybe you ever would have imagined
yourself to be similarly, you know, to to

00:11:39.810 --> 00:11:41.180
take Trumpism.

00:11:41.180 --> 00:11:47.050
There's a lot of folks who in 2017, if you
said, hey, you know, by 2020, this guy is

00:11:47.050 --> 00:11:53.740
going to be suggesting injecting bleach to
treat, you know, a disease that you haven't

00:11:53.740 --> 00:11:54.740
even heard about yet.

00:11:54.740 --> 00:11:57.040
A lot of people would have said that will
never happen.

00:11:57.040 --> 00:11:58.040
There's no way.

00:11:58.040 --> 00:11:59.040
But slowly we get there.

00:11:59.040 --> 00:12:02.529
We get there drawing on a hurricane map with
a Sharpie, and it gets more and more and more

00:12:02.529 --> 00:12:03.610
insane.

00:12:03.610 --> 00:12:08.200
Do you find that that takes place with the
recruitment of Scientologists?

00:12:08.200 --> 00:12:09.820
Speaker 5: Oh, absolutely.

00:12:09.820 --> 00:12:12.440
I mean, David, that's what my book is about.

00:12:12.440 --> 00:12:21.670
My book is about my immersion into the the
world of Scientology, to the point where I

00:12:21.670 --> 00:12:32.770
was as dedicated and as convinced that Scientology
was the only route, the only salvation for

00:12:32.770 --> 00:12:35.950
all of mankind, as anybody could be.

00:12:35.950 --> 00:12:41.070
I mean, I became the the international spokesperson
of Scientology.

00:12:41.070 --> 00:12:46.420
I was like the head of the dirty tricks department
that dealt with the enemies of Scientology.

00:12:46.420 --> 00:12:55.790
I was 100% convinced, not that I was doing
bad, but I was doing good, that I was saving

00:12:55.790 --> 00:13:02.019
the world, that I was saving every man, woman
and child on planet Earth, and that this was

00:13:02.019 --> 00:13:05.490
the most important thing that could possibly
be done.

00:13:05.490 --> 00:13:15.670
And I, I hope that anybody who reads the book
will understand the parallels between my experiences

00:13:15.670 --> 00:13:21.230
in Scientology and what they see with other
people who act insane.

00:13:21.230 --> 00:13:26.140
Speaker 1: What were some of the small asks
that that they start with?

00:13:26.140 --> 00:13:30.850
Speaker 5: Well, it really started for me
when I was a child and my parents got involved

00:13:30.850 --> 00:13:35.660
and they started saying to me, look, the solution
to this problem.

00:13:35.660 --> 00:13:40.410
Is contained in what Ron Hubbard says is the
answer to this problem.

00:13:40.410 --> 00:13:45.370
Do what Ron says and your world view becomes
l.

00:13:45.370 --> 00:13:52.420
Ron Hubbard solved every problem of every
person on earth and all of mankind and laid

00:13:52.420 --> 00:13:54.259
it all out for us.

00:13:54.259 --> 00:14:01.700
And if we're too stupid or too lazy to find
out what all those answers are and apply them,

00:14:01.700 --> 00:14:03.450
then more fool us.

00:14:03.450 --> 00:14:08.699
We need to be dedicated because he has come
up with the answers for everything.

00:14:08.699 --> 00:14:16.890
And as soon as you start down that path that
there is one person who has got the answers

00:14:16.890 --> 00:14:18.560
to everything.

00:14:18.560 --> 00:14:25.160
You then go into some of the answers and those
answers become themselves.

00:14:25.160 --> 00:14:29.730
The next step in that immersion process.

00:14:29.730 --> 00:14:35.889
You know, there are a lot of them in Scientology,
a big one, a huge one, is that you have to

00:14:35.889 --> 00:14:39.079
be responsible for your own condition.

00:14:39.079 --> 00:14:47.269
And this is a, you know, a platitude that
Hubbard came up with that has some applicability

00:14:47.269 --> 00:14:49.610
and validity to it.

00:14:49.610 --> 00:14:56.089
But when you take it to the extreme that it's
taken to in the Hubbard's circumstance, it

00:14:56.089 --> 00:15:02.889
means in Scientology, if you get run over
by a car, it's your responsibility.

00:15:02.889 --> 00:15:08.820
You are responsible for for the condition
that you that you got yourself into.

00:15:08.820 --> 00:15:15.770
And that means that at some previous time,
you ran over someone else with a car.

00:15:15.770 --> 00:15:16.770
Right.

00:15:16.770 --> 00:15:23.430
In order to feel the need to be punished and
punish yourself by getting run over by a car.

00:15:23.430 --> 00:15:24.430
Right.

00:15:24.430 --> 00:15:31.430
This is the ultimate in victim blaming and
it is also the ultimate in no matter what

00:15:31.430 --> 00:15:38.020
bad thing may happen to you in Scientology,
that Scientology may cause or do to you, it's

00:15:38.020 --> 00:15:39.020
your fault.

00:15:39.020 --> 00:15:41.069
It's not Scientology's fault, right?

00:15:41.069 --> 00:15:42.069
Speaker 1: Yeah.

00:15:42.069 --> 00:15:45.320
And of course, those are the types of arguments
that are completely falsifiable.

00:15:45.320 --> 00:15:49.020
There's absolutely no way to argue that that
it's untrue.

00:15:49.020 --> 00:15:54.660
The book is A Billion Years My Escape from
a Life in the Highest Ranks of Scientology.

00:15:54.660 --> 00:15:57.540
We've been speaking with the book's author,
Mike Rinder.

00:15:57.540 --> 00:15:59.441
Mike, really appreciate your time today.

00:15:59.441 --> 00:16:00.441
Speaker 5: Thank you, David.

00:16:00.441 --> 00:16:00.750
It's great to see you again.

